/**

@page page_exahype_numerical_scheme Adding a numerical scheme

\tableofcontents

Where @ref page_exahype_modelling_pde_solver "modelling the PDE solver" describes
*what* equation a solver evolves, a numerical scheme describes *how* it is
actually evolved: which algorithmic steps make up one time step (or one setup
or plotting phase), in which order, and with which dependencies between them.
This is the second, implementation-facing generator layer that the
@ref page_exahype_design_philosophy "design philosophy" page refers to.


# Solver.NumericalScheme

A concrete discretisation - Finite Volumes with a Rusanov Riemann solver and
explicit Euler time stepping, ADER-DG, Runge-Kutta Discontinuous Galerkin, ...
- is realised as a subclass of the abstract nested class
exahype2.api.Solver.NumericalScheme. It is one of three such contracts a
Solver plugs in (the other two being Solver.GridConstruction, for how the
mesh the solver runs on is built up, and Solver.Plotter, for how a solution is
written out); all three follow the same shape: a constructor that just stores
the owning solver, plus one abstract construct_output() method that the
concrete scheme has to implement.

For a numerical scheme, that method is

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def construct_output(self, exahype2_project, peano_project, time_step):
  ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where `time_step` is a peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph: the task graph of the
surrounding project's time stepping phase. Implementing a numerical scheme
therefore means populating this graph with peano4.api.taskgraph.Task
instances and the dependencies between them - it does not yet mean writing
any Peano action sets or C++ by hand (@see
@ref page_exahype_compute_graph "lowering into a compute graph" for what
happens to the graph afterwards).


# A worked example

exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler builds the task graph for a Finite
Volume time step over a block-structured mesh:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
reconstruction_task         = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Reconstruction",         entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
update_cell_task            = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Update cell",            entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
compute_cfl_condition_task  = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Compute CFL condition",  entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
project_on_faces_task       = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Project on faces",       entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
compute_time_step_size_task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Compute time step size", entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.GLOBAL)

update_cell_task.add_dependency(reconstruction_task)
compute_cfl_condition_task.add_dependency(update_cell_task)
project_on_faces_task.add_dependency(update_cell_task)
compute_time_step_size_task.add_dependency(compute_cfl_condition_task)

time_step.add_task(reconstruction_task)
time_step.add_task(update_cell_task)
time_step.add_task(compute_cfl_condition_task)
time_step.add_task(project_on_faces_task)
time_step.add_task(compute_time_step_size_task)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Every Task is tagged with the kind of mesh entity it works on
(Task.GridEntity.VERTEX/FACE/CELL/GLOBAL) and the tasks it depends on. A new
numerical scheme typically follows the same pattern: create one Task per
algorithmic sub-step, wire up add_dependency() calls to express the required
order, and add every task to the phase's Graph via add_task(). While
developing a scheme, Graph.visualise() renders the resulting graph with
matplotlib to sanity-check the dependency structure.

*/
